# 1. Purpose and Scope

This document specifies the in-application notification mechanism for the OPEX Budgeting System (OBS). It resolves OI-WP-03 — the open item raised during authoring of Functional Requirements: Workforce Planning v1.0 — which identified that the transfer workflow requires in-application notifications for transfer approval requests, rejections, and expiries, but that no notification delivery mechanism had been specified.

This specification also resolves OI-FAW-01 from Functional Requirements: Finance Approval Workflow v1.2, which identified that the five FAW notification touchpoints defined in FAW Section 8 were non-functional pending this mechanism.

## 1.1 Scope

This specification covers:

- Notification bell component — placement in the application shell header, unread count badge, and click behaviour.

- Notification inbox panel — layout, notification item rendering, read/unread state, mark-as-read interactions, and empty state.

- obs.notifications PostgreSQL table — full schema, write model, expiry model, and Claude Code notes.

- Notification service API — three endpoints: list notifications, mark one read, mark all read.

- Notification delivery rules — which events generate notifications, who receives them, and what content they contain. Covers all eight notification events across the Workforce Planning transfer workflow and Finance Approval Workflow.

- Deep-link routing — how notifications link back to the relevant screen within OBS.

- Acceptance criteria — testable conditions for all requirements in this document.

- Companion amendments status — all four companion amendments have been issued and are complete (see Section 9).

This specification does not cover:

- External email or SMS notifications — OBS delivers in-application notifications only. No email or SMS delivery is in scope.

- Push notifications to mobile devices — the OBS mobile experience is read-only; push notification infrastructure is not required.

- Notification preferences or opt-out controls — all users receive all notifications generated by events within their scope. No per-user notification preference management is in scope.

- Administrative notification management — Administrators do not have a view of other users' notification inboxes.

